          <figcaption class="ar-cap">From the supplied production environment: a T-shirt sewing line</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>This guide answers that. We review ten manufacturers positioned for different buyer profiles, then hand you the seven-point framework our sourcing team runs on every new factory. Use the list as a shortlist. Keep the framework</p>

        <h2 id="why-china-leads">Why China Still Leads Global T-Shirt Manufacturing</h2>

        <p>Three structural advantages hold, and none of them is cheap labor</p>

        <p><strong>Fabric supply density.</strong> Combed ring-spun jersey, slub, interlock, poly-spandex performance blends: most of it is milled within a few hundred kilometers of the sewing floors that consume it. That proximity is why a 400-piece lab dip run on a 180gsm jersey is even possible. Elsewhere, the mill won't open a dye lot that small</p>

        <p><strong>Vertical integration.</strong> A single industrial zone in Guangdong can cover spinning, dyeing, cutting, sewing, screen printing, embroidery, and packing. Move a chest print 2cm and nobody waits three weeks for blanks from a third country</p>

        <p><strong>Export logistics maturity.</strong> Shenzhen, Ningbo, and Shanghai sail apparel containers on fixed weekly schedules, and the forwarders there already know how a knit-garment commercial invoice, packing list, and certificate of origin need to read for US and EU customs</p>

        <p>For buyers, that adds up to MOQs stretching from a few hundred pieces per color to tens of thousands, production cycles that fit a seasonal calendar, and FOB pricing that still wins on a landed-cost basis once rework and rejection rates are priced in</p>

        <h2 id="seven-point-framework">How We Evaluated These T-Shirt Manufacturers (Our 7-Point Framework)</h2>

        <p>Every factory below was scored against seven criteria. Reuse the checklist on any supplier you find yourself</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Evaluation Dimension</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>How to Verify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Certifications &amp; compliance</td><td>Determines whether you can sell into EU/US retail and whether your brand carries social-audit risk</td><td>Request certificate numbers, check them on the issuing body's public registry</td></tr><tr><td>MOQ flexibility</td><td>Mismatched MOQ is one of the most common reasons small brands get rejected or over-order</td><td>Ask for MOQ per color, per style, and per fabric</td></tr><tr><td>Sampling capability</td><td>Sample quality predicts bulk quality more reliably than any sales pitch</td><td>Order a paid sample and time the turnaround</td></tr><tr><td>Production capacity</td><td>Capacity determines whether your reorder ships on schedule</td><td>Ask for monthly output by category, not a single total</td></tr><tr><td>OEM/ODM capability</td><td>ODM shortens development; OEM protects your exact spec</td><td>Confirm who owns the pattern and who develops the fit</td></tr><tr><td>Export experience</td><td>Experienced exporters handle documentation and inspection without hand-holding</td><td>Ask which markets they ship to and which incoterms they quote</td></tr><tr><td>Communication responsiveness</td><td>Time-zone friction kills projects more often than price</td><td>Send a technical question and measure reply quality, not just speed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>Run them in that order. Compliance and MOQ eliminate most mismatches before you've spent a dollar on sampling</p>

        <p>A word on certificates, because buyers mix these up constantly. BSCI and WRAP are social audits covering labor conditions and working hours. ISO 9001 certifies a quality management system, not the garment. OEKO-TEX STANDARD 100 covers chemical residues in the finished textile, tested against limit values for substances such as formaldehyde and azo dyes. A factory holding one and not the others is normal, not a red flag. For a plain-language breakdown before you start requesting documents, read our guide to <a href="/our-services/">apparel compliance certifications explained</a></p>

        <p>Unsure which criteria carry the most weight for your order? <a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our sourcing team</a> and we'll name the three dimensions that matter at your volume and in your target market</p>

        <h2 id="top-10">Top 10 T-Shirt Manufacturers in China (Reviewed)</h2>

        <p>The ten below sit at different points on the spectrum: small-batch specialists, high-volume knit exporters, and ODM-led developers. Profiles draw on published company information and our own sourcing work. Pull current MOQ, pricing, and certificate status from each factory before you commit</p>

        <h3>1. YAUUM – Best for Private-Label and Streetwear Programs</h3>

        <p>YAUUM is a B2B menswear manufacturer built around private-label and streetwear buyers: apparel brands, wholesalers, retailers, e-commerce sellers, and in-house sourcing teams. Six core categories run through the same workflow, hoodies, T-shirts, jackets, pants, sportswear, and streetwear</p>

        <p><strong>Core Strengths &amp; Capabilities</strong></p>

        <p>T-shirt programs sit alongside hoodie and streetwear production, which matters when you want a coordinated capsule instead of one orphan SKU. The <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">custom t-shirt manufacturing services</a> intake starts from a tech pack, reference photos, physical samples, target fabric, size range, label artwork, quantity, packaging spec, and destination market. Private-label detailing covers woven labels, neck and size labels, care labels, hang tags, stickers, barcodes, poly bags, and carton marks, all specified per project rather than forced into a house template</p>

        <p><strong>Certifications &amp; Compliance</strong></p>

        <p>Documentation is assembled per project against the destination market. Buyers shipping into EU or US retail should name the exact audit and chemical-safety certificates their channel requires at RFQ stage, not after sample approval</p>

        <p><strong>MOQ, Lead Time &amp; Capacity</strong></p>

        <p>MOQ and lead time are quoted per project, because fabric choice and decoration method drive both. For calibration: T-shirt MOQ in this segment typically lands at 300–500 pieces per color, sampling runs 7–14 days, bulk production 30–45 days, sea freight another 25–35 days. Treat those as planning ranges and check them against a live quote</p>

        <p><strong>Who It's Best For</strong></p>

        <p>Brands that want one supplier across T-shirts, hoodies, and streetwear, plus buyers who need private-label trim and packaging handled end to end</p>

        <h3>2. Shenzhou International Group – Best for Large-Scale Knit Production</h3>

        <p>Vertically integrated knitwear at the top of the Chinese market, running knitting through sewing inside one operational system and supplying major international sportswear labels. Minimums run into the multiple thousands per style, negotiated by contract. If your seasonal buy is forecast a year out and measured in containers, this is the tier that holds tolerance across it</p>

        <h3>3. Esquel Group – Best for High-End Cotton and Vertical Integration</h3>

        <p>A cotton specialist integrated from yarn to finished garment, which is where hand-feel and fiber traceability actually come from. Mid-to-large volume only, so startup quantities won't clear the threshold. Is cotton provenance part of your retail story? Then the higher price buys something your customer can be told about</p>

        <h3>4. Luen Thai Holdings – Best for Full-Service Apparel Programs</h3>

        <p>Hong Kong-headquartered with production spread across several Asian countries, bundling design support, sourcing, manufacturing, and logistics into one contract. Mid-to-large volume on negotiated terms, with lead time shifting depending on which site in the network builds the order. Brands tired of coordinating six suppliers across four categories tend to end up at this model</p>

        <h3>5. TAL Apparel – Best for Technical and Performance Knits</h3>

        <p>An engineering-led manufacturer with a long shirt and knitwear history, strong on seam construction, performance fabrics, and tolerance tables. Volume-oriented, suited to buyers working from an established demand forecast. The more technical your tech pack, the shorter the conversation gets</p>

        <h3>6. Youngor Group – Best for Integrated Vertical Manufacturing</h3>

        <p>A large integrated textile and garment enterprise where fabric and garment sit inside the same company, which shows up as color consistency on repeat orders rather than as a line on a brochure. Medium and large volume; small startup runs are not the model. Reordering the same shade three seasons running is the scenario this suits</p>

        <h3>7. Bosideng International – Best for Outerwear-Adjacent Knit Programs</h3>

        <p>Best known for down outerwear, with the seasonal collection infrastructure to carry knit tops alongside it. Volume-oriented and negotiated per program. Worth approaching when your tee line is one component of a wider range, less so when it is the whole order</p>

        <h3>8. Ever-Glory International Group – Best for Mid-Volume Export Programs</h3>

        <p>A Nanjing-based listed apparel OEM/ODM exporter with long-running experience shipping into Europe, the US, and Japan. Mid-volume minimums, and lead times that fit standard seasonal planning. Think of it as the middle ground between a twenty-machine workshop and a mega-factory that never answers your email</p>

        <h3>9. Jiangsu Hongdou – Best for Cost-Efficient Bulk Basics</h3>

        <p>An established Chinese apparel enterprise with broad capacity in basic knitwear, where unit economics rather than development support make the case. Bulk-oriented minimums. Buyers whose margin model lives or dies on cost per piece will see the logic immediately; everyone else should look higher up this list</p>

        <h3>10. Smaller Specialized Knit Workshops (Guangdong &amp; Zhejiang Clusters) – Best for Low MOQ and Fast Sampling</h3>

        <p>Past the named manufacturers, Guangdong and Zhejiang host hundreds of specialized knit workshops serving small brands, DTC sellers, and Amazon FBA operators</p>

        <p><strong>Core Strengths &amp; Capabilities</strong></p>

        <p>Low MOQ, fast sampling, and genuine flexibility on decoration, screen print, DTG, puff print, embroidery. You trade away vertical integration and consistent compliance maturity to get it</p>

        <p><strong>Certifications &amp; Compliance</strong></p>

        <p>Wildly variable. Some hold valid social audits, many hold none. Verification matters more here than anywhere else on this list</p>

        <p><strong>MOQ, Lead Time &amp; Capacity</strong></p>

        <p>Minimums often start in the low hundreds per color. Sampling is quick, bulk scheduling is less predictable, especially from August through October</p>

        <p><strong>Who It's Best For</strong></p>

        <p>Startups, DTC brands, and buyers testing designs before scaling. For low-minimum runs with full private-label detailing, see our <a href="/custom-t-shirt-manufacturer-guide/">low MOQ t-shirt manufacturing</a> options</p>

