        <h2 id="batch-vs-bulk">Small Batch vs. Bulk vs. ODM: Which Type of Manufacturer Do You Need?</h2>

        <p>Most sourcing frustration traces back to a scale mismatch, not a bad factory. Match the manufacturer type to your real order profile</p>

        <p><strong>Small batch, roughly 100–500 pieces per color.</strong> You're testing designs, validating demand, or running a DTC drop. Weight sampling speed, low minimums, and decoration flexibility above unit cost, because you will pay more per piece and that's fine. Two failure modes dominate: approaching a mega-factory and getting ignored, or ordering 2,000 pieces to hit a MOQ when your sell-through supports 400. <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">Private label t-shirt manufacturing</a> with low minimums is the right entry point at this stage</p>

        <p><strong>Mid to bulk, roughly 1,000–10,000+ pieces per style.</strong> Demand is proven and you now need cost efficiency and schedule reliability. Weight capacity, compliance documentation, and reorder consistency. The trap is taking the lowest quote and discovering at 5,000 pieces that the factory can't repeat the sample, usually showing up as a half-size drift in body length or a shade shift between dye lots</p>

        <p><strong>ODM, where the factory develops design and patterns.</strong> You want speed to market and you're willing to build on the factory's existing blocks. Weight design capability, pattern ownership terms, and exclusivity. Buyers routinely assume ODM means they own the design. They often don't. Get pattern ownership, and whether the style can be sold to a competitor, in writing before you approve the sample</p>

        <p>A working rule: under 500 pieces per color, skip the vertically integrated mega-factory. Over 5,000, don't run a small workshop without third-party inspection booked</p>

        <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common Mistakes When Sourcing T-Shirts from China (And How to Avoid Them)</h2>

        <p>These surface in nearly every sourcing conversation we have</p>

        <p><strong>Chasing the lowest unit price.</strong> A quote that undercuts the field by 20% is usually hiding something specific: 160gsm fabric instead of 180gsm, lower stitch density, single-needle hems where you specified double, or a subcontracted sewing floor nobody told you about. Normalize every quote to identical fabric weight and construction, or you're comparing two different garments. The same mistake shows up at the other end of the deal, when FOB gets treated as landed cost. Sea freight, insurance, duty, brokerage, and last-mile delivery all land on top before you set a retail price</p>

        <p><strong>Skipping the paid sample and leaving GSM out of the brief.</strong> "Cotton T-shirt" specifies nothing. A 180gsm combed ring-spun crew neck and a 140gsm carded jersey are different products at different prices and different retail positions. Fix the spec first, then pay for a sample and judge the garment in your hands rather than the photo in the chat window. A factory that can't nail the sample won't nail the bulk</p>

        <p><strong>Misreading certification scope.</strong> A valid BSCI audit says nothing about chemical residues in your fabric. Match each certificate to what your selling channel actually asks for</p>

        <p><strong>Running on verbal agreements.</strong> MOQ, lead time, measurement tolerance, payment terms, AQL level: put all of it on paper. A one-page PO with clear specs prevents the large majority of disputes we see</p>

        <h2 id="verification">How to Verify a T-Shirt Manufacturer Before You Order</h2>

        <p>Verification is a sequence, not a gut feeling. Work it in this order</p>

        <ol class="ar-list"><li><strong>Request certificate numbers, not certificate images.</strong> Take the number to the issuing body's public registry and check it there. Images get edited; registry entries don't</li><li><strong>Ask for a live video walkthrough.</strong> A scheduled call panning across the sewing floor, cutting tables, and QC station tells you more in ten minutes than a photo gallery ever will</li><li><strong>Run a trial order.</strong> Produce 100–300 pieces before you commit to the full run. This is how you learn the real lead time, the real defect rate, and how they behave when something goes wrong</li><li><strong>Book pre-shipment inspection.</strong> For bulk, a recognized third-party agency inspecting against your AQL catches defects while the goods are still in China</li><li><strong>Establish who actually sews the order.</strong> Plenty of suppliers broker production out. Ask whether it's in-house or outsourced, and ask early</li></ol>

        <p>Want to run this sequence on a live RFQ? <a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our team</a> and we will walk the verification steps with you</p>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ: Sourcing T-Shirts from China</h2>

          <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the MOQ for t-shirt manufacturing in China?</h3></summary><p>MOQ varies by factory type. Large vertically integrated manufacturers often require several thousand pieces per style, while specialized workshops and flexible suppliers commonly start around 300–500 pieces per color. Fabric choice and decoration method can raise the minimum. Always confirm MOQ per color, per style, and per fabric, since those three numbers often differ</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does it take to manufacture custom t-shirts in China?</h3></summary><p>Typical timelines run 7–14 days for sampling, 30–45 days for bulk production after sample approval, and 25–35 days for sea freight to the US or EU. Air freight cuts shipping to roughly 5–10 days at higher cost. Peak season, around August to October, can add one to two weeks. These are common market ranges, so confirm against your specific order</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How much does it cost to manufacture t-shirts in China?</h3></summary><p>FOB pricing commonly falls around $2.8–6.5 per piece for a 180gsm combed cotton crew neck at a 3,000-piece order, depending on fabric, decoration, and construction. Organic cotton, heavyweight fleece, and complex printing push the upper end. These are industry reference ranges, so request a live quote for your exact spec</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What certifications should a Chinese t-shirt manufacturer have?</h3></summary><p>It depends on your market. For EU and US retail, buyers typically look for social compliance audits such as BSCI or WRAP, a quality management certification such as ISO 9001, and chemical safety certification such as OEKO-TEX for the finished fabric. No single certificate covers everything, so match the documentation to your channel's requirements</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I get samples before placing a bulk order?</h3></summary><p>Yes, and you should. Most manufacturers provide paid samples, with costs often credited against the bulk order. Sampling typically takes 7–14 days. Treat the sample as a quality test, not a formality, and evaluate fabric weight, stitching, print durability, and measurements against your spec</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What's the difference between OEM and ODM for t-shirts?</h3></summary><p>OEM means you supply the design, tech pack, and specifications, and the factory manufactures to your spec. ODM means the factory offers existing designs and patterns that you brand as your own. OEM gives you control and exclusivity; ODM gives you speed and lower development cost. Confirm pattern ownership before choosing ODM</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I verify a t-shirt manufacturer is legitimate?</h3></summary><p>Verify certificate numbers on the issuing body's registry, request a live video walkthrough of the production floor, run a small trial order, and book third-party inspection for bulk. Also confirm whether production is in-house or subcontracted. Legitimate factories answer these requests directly</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Do Chinese t-shirt manufacturers ship internationally?</h3></summary><p>Most established manufacturers and exporters ship worldwide, quoting FOB, CIF, or DDP depending on your preference. They typically work with freight forwarders for sea, air, and express shipping. Confirm which incoterms they quote and who handles customs documentation before you finalize terms</p></details>

